# Hostile audit — complement outer-train bracing energy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:**
- `research/target_c_complement_outer_train_bracing_energy_2026-09-15.md`
- `research/verify_target_c_complement_outer_train_bracing_energy.py`

**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only full-core Type-II scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The candidate adds a second outer-detour payment from trains outside the dominant
atomic orbit.  The highest-risk points are not the scalar calculation; they are:

1. whether Section 157 really turns two-sided diameter bracing into a statement about
   the angular interval between outer terminals;
2. whether outer-pocket retraction preserves the noncrossing chord order used in the
   packing lemma;
3. whether the round-hull perimeter measure gives the claimed endpoint-capacity
   constant `1/2`; and
4. whether dominant and complement energy payments are genuinely disjoint rather than
   two uses of the same detour currency.

All four checks pass, with the scope restrictions recorded below.

---

## 1. Complement two-outer population: count audit

Let `M` be the absolute train mass in the frozen dominant `pi/3` orbit and `C` the
all-train scalar.  Audited §142/168 gives

\[
M\ge4\Delta_2/C.
\]

The number of complete trains outside the dominant windows is

\[
N_c={T\over D}-M+o(1)
=C+{t+\kappa\over2}-M+o(1).
\]

Since `C<=U` and `C-4Delta2/C` is strictly increasing,

\[
N_c\le U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U}+o(1).
\]

The joint orbit-capacity theorem applies to the actual dominant-window outer
occurrence count `K`, so

\[
K\le Q(\delta):=\Phi^{-1}(\delta).
\]

Thus complement trains carry at least `pi+delta-Q(delta)` outer occurrences.  If
`P_c` complement trains have two outer occurrences, then all other complement trains
have at most one, giving

\[
P_c\ge
\pi+\delta-Q(\delta)-N_c.
\]

No assumption that complement mass is non-atomic is used; it includes all trains
outside the frozen dominant-orbit windows.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 2. Fractional pair retention loses at most `delta`

On route `i`, the number `c_i` of selected complement endpoints satisfies

\[
c_i\le\ell_i=s_i+d_i.
\]

Retain endpoint weight at most `s_i`, discarding no more than

\[
(c_i-s_i)_+\le d_i.
\]

If the two endpoint weights of one train are `a,b`, the retained pair weight
`min(a,b)` loses at most `(1-a)+(1-b)`.  Therefore total pair-weight loss is no more
than the total endpoint-weight loss, hence at most

\[
\sum_i d_i=d_{out}=\delta D.
\]

This is valid even for bridge occurrences: the outer walk and `ell_i` are
occurrence-counted, so a bridge traversed twice supplies two endpoint occurrences and
two route-energy occurrences.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 3. Physical perimeter measure has density `1/2`

For the diameter-`D` disk, radius is `D/2`.  An oriented normal-angle increment
`dtheta` carries boundary length `(D/2)dtheta`; after division by `D`, the limiting
physical perimeter measure on `R/2pi Z` is

\[
{1\over2}d\theta.
\]

The Type-II near-disk convergence used in audited §142 gives the same weak limit for
the loaded force hull.  Because the limit distribution is continuous, the cumulative
distribution convergence is uniform over angular intervals.  Thus a moving interval
of angular width `A` carries normalized side-length mass at most `A/2+o(1)`.

The finite hull may have one side containing several retained terminal occurrences and
all those occurrences are assigned the same side-normal atom.  Canonical Type-II
interface collapse gives `max s_i=o(D)`, so no single such atom has macroscopic mass;
the uniform interval statement remains valid in the limit.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 4. Outer-pocket retraction preserves cyclic order

Sections 67/126 add every missing loaded-hull side and identify the Section-63 route
`W_i` with the boundary chain of the corresponding convex outer pocket.  Distinct
pocket interiors are disjoint.  Therefore each outer terminal midpoint can be pushed
through its own pocket to the loaded-hull side without passing another terminal or
crossing a train midpoint curve.  Along each route, use the original occurrence order
as an infinitesimal tie-breaker when several terminals retract to the same side.

Consequently a family of train midpoint arcs that is pairwise noncrossing before
retraction gives a nonalternating endpoint pairing on the convex hull boundary after
retraction.  Collapsing a whole side to its normal angle can create ties, but not a
macroscopic order reversal; `max s_i=o(D)` makes tie mass negligible.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 5. Same-sector train curves are noncrossing

Audited §142 proves that a strict-rhombus crossing between two trains requires their
projective directions to differ by strictly more than `pi/3`.  Partitioning the
projective phase circle into three half-open sectors of width `pi/3` therefore makes
each sector family crossing-free.

Train midpoint segments lie inside strict-rhombus faces.  Distinct train curves cannot
have a transverse geometric intersection outside a common strict rhombus, because
face interiors are disjoint and a contact edge belongs to one train.  Hence
"no train crossing" is the topological noncrossing condition needed by the chord
argument.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 6. Section 157 really forces a phase zero into either shore interval

Fix an outer-to-outer train `tau` and choose either one of its Jordan shores as `S`.
Section 157 is symmetric under exchanging the two shores, so its two-sign conclusion
holds for the shore whose convex-hull boundary arc has smaller normal-angle width
`w<=pi`.

Every actual diameter endpoint belongs to the loaded force-hull set in the canonical
C65 normalization.  Type-II near-disk convergence gives a common center `o_D` such
that uniformly for loaded points

\[
|p-o_D|=D/2+o(D).
\]

If `xy` is an actual diameter and `y` is its endpoint in `S`, then

\[
{p_y-p_x\over D}=n_y+o(1),
\]

where `n_y` is the outward radial direction at `y`.  This follows by expanding the
identity `|p_y-p_x|=D` inside the `D/2+o(D)` annulus: the endpoint radial directions
must be antipodal up to `o(1)`.

Section 157 supplies crossing diameter vectors oriented into `S` with both signs of
`r_h dot Ju`.  Therefore the radial normals of loaded endpoints in `S` occur with both
signs of `n dot Ju`.  Loaded hull vertices in `S` occupy the single convex-hull arc
between the two retracted train terminals.  By continuity, that normal-angle interval
contains a zero of `n dot Ju`.

The zeros occur at normal angles equal, modulo `pi`, to the train contact phase.  Thus
one representative of the projective train phase lies in the terminal normal-angle
interval, up to `o(1)`.

This is the decisive geometric step.  It uses **actual diameter bracing**, not merely
the train cut topology.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 7. Terminal energy formula is correct

Normalize the smaller shore normal interval to `[0,w]`, `0<=w<=pi`, and put the
train phase representative at `phi in[0,w]`.  The two hull tangent directions are
`pi/2` and `w+pi/2` projectively.  An outer terminal contact edge has projective phase
`phi`, while its exact oriented route energy is at least its projective energy.  Hence

\[
E_{term}
\ge2-\sin\phi-\sin(w-\phi)
\ge2-2\sin(w/2).
\]

For antipodal deficit `r=pi-w`, this equals

\[
2-2\cos(r/2).
\]

At `w=pi` the lower bound is zero, as it must be for an exactly antipodal pair; at a
local return `w=0` it is two.  The endpoint behavior is geometrically consistent.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 8. Near-antipodal capacity lemma: laminar audit

Take one same-sector noncrossing family and retain only chords with antipodal deficit
`r<=r_0<pi/3`.  Each chord's unique shorter boundary arc then has length

\[
>2pi/3.
\]

For noncrossing chords, these shorter arcs are laminar: two partially overlapping,
non-nested shorter arcs would have alternating endpoints and hence crossing chords.

There are at most two top-level disjoint shorter arcs, because three arcs each longer
than `2pi/3` cannot be disjoint on a circle of length `2pi`.  Inside one top-level arc,
two disjoint children are impossible because the parent has length at most `pi` while
two children would have combined length greater than `4pi/3`.  Thus each top-level
component is one nested chain.

For a nested chain, write the left endpoint span as `A` and right endpoint span as
`B`.  The outermost shorter arc minus the innermost shorter arc has length exactly
`A+B`; both arc lengths lie in `[pi-r_0,pi]`, so

\[
A+B\le r_0.
\]

If the chain has pair weight `m`, each endpoint set has weight `m` and is dominated by
`(1/2)dtheta`; hence `A>=2m-o(1)` and `B>=2m-o(1)`.  Thus

\[
m\le r_0/4+o(1).
\]

Two top-level chains give total weight at most `r_0/2+o(1)`.

Exact-diameter chords (`r=0`) make the shorter-arc choice nonunique, but two distinct
exact diameter chords cross.  A noncrossing sector contains at most one, whose
normalized weight is `o(1)`, so it does not affect the limiting lemma.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 9. Integration and the three-sector cost

For one sector family of mass `m<=pi/6`, the capacity lemma gives the deficit quantile
bound `r(s)>=2s`.  Since

\[
B(r)=2-2\cos(r/2)
\]

is increasing,

\[
E_{sector}
\ge\int_0^m B(2s)ds
=2m-2\sin m.
\]

This function is convex on the relevant interval.  If all three sector masses are at
most `pi/6`, Jensen gives

\[
E_{comp}\ge2p-6\sin(p/3)=J(p).
\]

If one sector exceeds `pi/6`, retaining only `pi/6` in that sector costs at least

\[
E(pi/6)=pi/3-1=0.04719\ldots .
\]

For the only range used in the scalar theorem, `p<=1`, this exceeds `J(p)` even at
`p=1`.  Hence the displayed `J(p)` bound is valid without assuming balanced sector
masses.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 10. Dominant and complement payments are disjoint

The additive dominant payment uses only fractionally retained outer occurrences whose
trains lie in the frozen dominant-orbit windows.  The complement bracing payment uses
terminal occurrences of trains **outside** those windows.  An outer edge occurrence
belongs to exactly one complete train.  Therefore no occurrence contributes to both
energy sums.

Both retention arguments use the scalar `delta` to upper-bound how much endpoint or
occurrence mass can exceed hull-side length capacity.  That does not itself count
energy; it only lowers the retained masses.  Adding the actual weighted edge-energy
lower bounds after retention is therefore legitimate.

This distinction is why the candidate adds the older `F(z)` dominant payment rather
than the joint `Phi(K)` payment: `Phi` can use total route detour to pay occurrence
excess and would not be manifestly disjoint from complement energy.  The `F(z)` term
is an actual selected dominant-edge energy sum.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 11. Scalar minimization audit

For

\[
z_*=
pi-t-kappa-2U+8Delta_2/U
\]

and

\[
p_*=
pi-Q(delta)-U-(t+kappa)/2+4Delta_2/U,
\]

the margin is

\[
M=F(z_*)+J(p_*)-delta.
\]

### `kappa`

`dz_*/dkappa=4Delta_2/U^2>0`.  Also

\[
{d\over d\kappa}
\left(U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U}\right)
=-{2\Delta_2\over U^2}<0,
\]

so `p_*` increases with `kappa`.  Both energy terms increase.  Worst case is
`kappa=0`.

### `delta`

On the claimed interval the certificate verifies

\[
z_*<1.33,
\quad p_*>0.61,
\quad Q(delta)<1.62.
\]

Hence

\[
F'(z_*)<0.025,
\qquad dz_*/d\delta<8,
\]

while

\[
Q'(delta)>25,
\qquad -dN_*/d\delta<4,
\]

so `dp_*/ddelta<-21`.  Finally

\[
J'(p_*)>J'(0.61)>0.041.
\]

Therefore

\[
{dM\over d\delta}
<0.2-21(0.041)-1<-1.6.
\]

Worst case is maximum detour `delta=R(t)/2`, where `U=C0`.

The one-variable boundary margin is strictly decreasing in `t`; Pyroam independently
reproduced

\[
M(1.82806)=2.95119\times10^{-5}>0,
\]

\[
M(1.8280659)=7.17\times10^{-8}>0,
\]

\[
M(1.828066)<0.
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 12. Final disposition

The proof-only implication survives hostile review:

```text
dominant atomic orbit
 -> retained dominant outer-edge energy F(z)

joint orbit capacity
 -> upper bound on dominant outer terminal count
 -> many complement trains must be outer-to-outer

Section 157 two-sided actual-diameter bracing
 + three pi/3 noncrossing phase sectors
 + round-hull endpoint capacity
 -> independent complement terminal energy J(p)

F(z)+J(p) > total available outer detour
 -> exclude t <= 1.82806.
```

The result does not close C-II.  It is, however, qualitatively stronger than the
preceding scalar refinements because it couples the atomic crossing phase to the
**actual diameter-braced complement train population**.

**Final verdict:** PASS at proof-only full-core Type-II scope.  C-II and Level I remain
open.
